\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.1}{\ignorespaces Characteristic frequency family of the vertical axial-flow pump at \(960\,\mathrm {r/min}\). The dashed line is the supply frequency reported as interference in the healthy spectrum.}}{12}{figure.caption.4}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.2}{\ignorespaces Shaft-synchronous reference at \(16\,\mathrm {Hz}\). A record of \(8192\) samples at \(10\,\mathrm {kHz}\) lasts \(0.8192\,\mathrm {s}\) and contains about \(13.1\) revolutions.}}{12}{figure.caption.5}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.3}{\ignorespaces The diagnostic chain addressed by this paper. Chapter~\ref {ch:previous} covers the forces and the sensor. Chapter~\ref {ch:methods} covers the two computational blocks. Chapter~\ref {ch:estimation} covers the rig that produces the acceleration and the estimate of the label.}}{26}{figure.caption.6}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces Radial vibration severity reported by You et al.\ for the four operating states. These are published measurements, not quantities computed from the illustrative signals.}}{29}{figure.caption.7}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.2}{\ignorespaces Illustrative radial acceleration over the first \(200\,\mathrm {ms}\). Each record is scaled so that the RMS of its high-pass-integrated velocity equals the published severity of that state.}}{30}{figure.caption.8}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.3}{\ignorespaces One step of the Mallat analysis bank. The detail coefficients at level \(j\) are the dyadic wavelet transform at scale \(2^{j}\).}}{31}{figure.caption.9}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.4}{\ignorespaces Fraction of energy by dyadic level for the illustrative signals, db4, eight levels. The bars are a partition of each synthetic record. They are not the energy table of the laboratory records, which the source publishes only as plots.}}{32}{figure.caption.10}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.5}{\ignorespaces Normalized baseband spectra of the illustrative accelerations. Vertical lines mark \(8\), \(16\), \(32\), \(48\), and \(64\,\mathrm {Hz}\). Healthy energy favors \(f_{0}\) and its low harmonics; the eccentric state is dominated by \(f_{0}\); the pedestal and seal-wear states carry a stronger half-order.}}{35}{figure.caption.11}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.6}{\ignorespaces Data flow of the VWC map. Severity and the spectral block meet only at the concatenation.}}{37}{figure.caption.12}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.7}{\ignorespaces Hard-margin geometry in a feature plane. The solid line is the decision boundary. The dashed lines bound the slab. Only points on the dashed lines are support vectors.}}{38}{figure.caption.13}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.8}{\ignorespaces The modified leap. The new position of the worst frog is a convex combination, with random contractions, of the step toward the local best and the step toward the global best. The weights depend on distance to the global best.}}{43}{figure.caption.14}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.9}{\ignorespaces Outer loop of MSFLA-SVM. The SVM solver is inside the fitness block. The test set is used only in the final block.}}{46}{figure.caption.15}%
